WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.444 --> 00:00:03.225
Imagine you're running a database for
a soccer club.

2
00:00:03.225 --> 00:00:07.326
As a database engineer, there's a lot of
work required to manage this database.

3
00:00:07.326 --> 00:00:08.059
For example,

4
00:00:08.059 --> 00:00:11.916
you might need to categorize players
into groups according to their ages.

5
00:00:11.916 --> 00:00:14.237
How can you complete this kind of task?

6
00:00:14.237 --> 00:00:18.756
You can use sequel comparison
operators over the next few minutes.

7
00:00:18.756 --> 00:00:23.151
You'll learn how to explain the concept
of sequel comparison operators and

8
00:00:23.151 --> 00:00:26.247
utilize sequel comparison
operators in a database.

9
00:00:26.247 --> 00:00:29.211
So what our sequel comparison operators,

10
00:00:29.211 --> 00:00:32.999
Comparison operators are used
to compare two values or

11
00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:37.629
expressions where the outcome
result can be either true or false.

12
00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:42.270
They can be used to filter data and
to include and exclude data.

13
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:45.023
So how are these operators used in sequel?

14
00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:50.318
Sequel uses common mathematical
comparison operators by

15
00:00:50.318 --> 00:00:57.448
means of the symbols equal to less than
and greater than it also uses less than or

16
00:00:57.448 --> 00:01:02.002
equal to greater than or
equal to and not equal to.

17
00:01:02.002 --> 00:01:05.234
Now let's explore how to use
these comparison operators and

18
00:01:05.234 --> 00:01:07.271
relevant symbols in a practical way.

19
00:01:07.271 --> 00:01:11.719
Using a database to demonstrate the use
of sequel comparison operators,

20
00:01:11.719 --> 00:01:15.823
I'll use the example of an employee
table from a company database.

21
00:01:15.823 --> 00:01:22.747
This table includes information on
each employee's ID, name and salary.

22
00:01:22.747 --> 00:01:26.443
Now let's assume the employer wants
to extract relevant data from

23
00:01:26.443 --> 00:01:30.734
the employee table about the employee's
salaries for different purposes.

24
00:01:30.734 --> 00:01:35.008
Each instance of data extraction will
require a different comparison operator.

25
00:01:35.008 --> 00:01:39.385
So in the first example the employer
wants to identify all employees

26
00:01:39.385 --> 00:01:42.931
receiving a salary Equal
to $18,000 per year.

27
00:01:42.931 --> 00:01:46.815
I can identify these employees
using the equal to operator.

28
00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:50.322
First I click the sequel
tab in the main menu.

29
00:01:50.322 --> 00:01:55.878
Then I write select asterix from employee

30
00:01:55.878 --> 00:02:01.918
where salary equal to symbol 18,000.

31
00:02:01.918 --> 00:02:04.520
Let's break this sequel statement down.

32
00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:07.155
The select command is
used to retrieve data.

33
00:02:07.155 --> 00:02:11.551
The asterix star denotes that I'm
selecting data from all columns,

34
00:02:11.551 --> 00:02:16.268
the from keyword and the table name
specify where the data will come from.

35
00:02:16.268 --> 00:02:19.501
And then I define the condition
using the wear clause.

36
00:02:19.501 --> 00:02:24.404
In this case, the condition uses the equal
to symbol to check if the salary

37
00:02:24.404 --> 00:02:28.530
value in each Record of
the table is equal to $18,000.

38
00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.273
If the result is true,
then the data is retrieved.

39
00:02:32.273 --> 00:02:36.044
So run the query and generate an output.

40
00:02:36.044 --> 00:02:39.920
The Output result of this query
is that the employees Carl and

41
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.428
John earned $18,000 per year.

42
00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:45.590
You can apply the other comparison
operators in a similar way

43
00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.393
to perform different functions.

44
00:02:47.393 --> 00:02:50.047
Let's take another
example to find out more.

45
00:02:50.047 --> 00:02:54.652
In this next example, the employer needs
to know which employees are receiving

46
00:02:54.652 --> 00:02:57.827
a salary That is less
than 20 $4,000 per year.

47
00:02:57.827 --> 00:03:00.671
This task requires a different operator.

48
00:03:00.671 --> 00:03:03.850
To find this information,

49
00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:09.240
I can write select asterix
from employee where

50
00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:13.947
salary less than symbol 24,000.

51
00:03:13.947 --> 00:03:18.818
This sequel statement utilizes the less
than symbol to check whether the value

52
00:03:18.818 --> 00:03:23.037
stored in each field of salary
column is less than $24,000.

53
00:03:23.037 --> 00:03:28.642
Once again, I select the gold button to
execute the query and generate an output.

54
00:03:28.642 --> 00:03:30.561
The output of this query
is that the employees?

55
00:03:30.561 --> 00:03:34.379
Carolyn john earned less than $24,000.

56
00:03:34.379 --> 00:03:38.283
Let's take another example where
the employer needs to determine which

57
00:03:38.283 --> 00:03:43.028
employees receive a salary that is less
than or equal to 24,000 dollars per year.

58
00:03:43.028 --> 00:03:48.157
In this case I need to
write the following query,

59
00:03:48.157 --> 00:03:53.912
select asterix from employee
where salary less than or

60
00:03:53.912 --> 00:03:57.306
equal to symbol 24,000.

61
00:03:57.306 --> 00:04:01.679
The only thing in this statement that
has changed from the previous example is

62
00:04:01.679 --> 00:04:06.187
the operator symbol this less than or
equal to symbol tells the sequel statement

63
00:04:06.187 --> 00:04:10.759
to check whether the value stored in each
field of the salary column is less than Or

64
00:04:10.759 --> 00:04:12.335
equal to $24,000.

65
00:04:12.335 --> 00:04:15.284
I click the go button
to execute the query.

66
00:04:15.284 --> 00:04:19.643
The Output results show that there
are four employees who Earn less than or

67
00:04:19.643 --> 00:04:21.905
equal to 24 $1,000 per year.

68
00:04:21.905 --> 00:04:26.467
What if The employer wants to know which
employees receive a salary that is greater

69
00:04:26.467 --> 00:04:28.860
than or equal to $24,000 per year.

70
00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.794
To generate these results,
I can use the greater than or

71
00:04:31.794 --> 00:04:34.048
equal to operator in my sequel statement.

72
00:04:34.048 --> 00:04:39.865
So I write the following sequel query
select asterix from employee where

73
00:04:39.865 --> 00:04:45.012
salary greater than or
equal to symbol 24,000 this time,

74
00:04:45.012 --> 00:04:50.445
the greater than or equal to symbol
is used to check whether the value

75
00:04:50.445 --> 00:04:57.343
stored in each field of the salary column
is greater Than or equal to $24,000.

76
00:04:57.343 --> 00:05:02.282
I click go to execute the query and
the output shows that there are three

77
00:05:02.282 --> 00:05:06.059
employees Who earn $24,000 or
more per year.

78
00:05:06.059 --> 00:05:11.440
The final comparison operator available
in sequel is a not equal to operator.

79
00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:16.176
In this final example, The employer wants
to know which employees receive a salary

80
00:05:16.176 --> 00:05:18.825
that is not equal to $24,000 per year.

81
00:05:18.825 --> 00:05:23.965
I can determine this using
the following sequel code,

82
00:05:23.965 --> 00:05:28.223
select asterix from employee where salary.

83
00:05:28.223 --> 00:05:32.555
Then I type less than and
greater than symbols to denote the not

84
00:05:32.555 --> 00:05:36.068
equal to operation and then I type 24,000.

85
00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:40.999
As with the previous operators, the sequel
statement utilizes the operator to check

86
00:05:40.999 --> 00:05:44.023
the value stored in each
field of the salary column.

87
00:05:44.023 --> 00:05:49.037
In this case, it Checks for
values that do not equate to $24,000.

88
00:05:49.037 --> 00:05:52.301
The output results of this
query show that there are three

89
00:05:52.301 --> 00:05:56.248
employees whose salaries are not
equal to $24,000 per year.

90
00:05:56.248 --> 00:05:59.135
You should now be able to
describe comparison operators and

91
00:05:59.135 --> 00:06:01.240
use them in a database in sequel.

92
00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:04.360
Congratulations on building
another important database skill